#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
tools/issue/oc-issue-dispatch — mechanized fork issue vetting, lane selection, and dispatch.

Usage:
  oc-issue-dispatch --auto [--dry-run] [--repo <path>] [--ledger <file>]
  oc-issue-dispatch <issue-number> [--to <session-uuid>] [--dry-run] [--repo <path>] [--ledger <file>]
  oc-issue-dispatch --selftest
  oc-issue-dispatch --help

--auto eligibility filters (each bypassable, see the flags below):
  claim-gated/parked  an issue carrying claim-gate markers
  LANDED (#337)       the fix already shipped -- a fork-space commit on
                      origin/main references it, OR the ledger holds a
                      done/close row targeting it.  --allow-landed bypasses.
  ALREADY-DISPATCHED  the issue was wired to a lane within the last 7 days and
  (#337)              not yet acked.  --redispatch bypasses.
  REOPENED (#414)     an issue GitHub reports with stateReason=REOPENED OVERRIDES
                      the LANDED and ALREADY-DISPATCHED arms -- both are
                      INFERENCES about state (an Issue-Ref trailer on
                      origin/main, or this tool's own past dispatch) and a
                      reopen is a deliberate act that contradicts them.  No flag
                      needed; the summary reports each override against the arm
                      it defeated.

Environment overrides (liveness, #451):
  OC_DISPATCH_NOTIFY_TIMEOUT  per-notify wall-clock budget in seconds (default 120).
                              The tool KILLS a notify at this bound and reports it
                              as UNVERIFIED, never FAILED -- see dispatch_to_lane.
  OC_DISPATCH_RECEIPT_GRACE   seconds to keep re-reading session-notify.journal for
                              a receipt after the send (default 30).
  OC_DISPATCH_MOCK_ISSUES     path to a JSON file of issue objects, replacing BOTH
                              GitHub reads (the open-issue list and the single-issue
                              lookup). The offline seam for the #483 fetch paths.

The open-issue list is a WINDOW (OPEN_ISSUES_LIMIT), never an exhaustive set: a
targeted dispatch for a number outside it resolves that ONE issue by direct
lookup, and REFUSES when even that fails. A stub is never constructed, because a
fabricated title with an EMPTY body makes the claim gate and the affinity
scoring evaluate nothing (#483).

Lane selection reads an issue's domains in TWO arms (#551). The DECLARED
`type(scope):` prefix is read FIRST, because it is a deliberate statement of what
the issue is about; the prose-keyword arm is the FALLBACK, for the many issues
that carry no prefix or whose scope names nothing we hold affinity for. A scope
is a DECLARATION, not a mention, so a title's own negation does not cancel it.
The two arms are a UNION: the declaration can only ADD a domain, never remove
one, which is what keeps a prefix-less issue reading exactly as it always did.

A dispatch's ceiling is therefore the notify budget PLUS the receipt grace (150s
by default), on top of the gh/roster reads that precede it. An outer `timeout`
shorter than that kills the run MID-SEND -- which is how a slow run was reported
as hanging "with no output and no side effect". Two behaviours address that: the
tool announces its budget before every send, and it line-buffers stdout, so a
killed run leaves the evidence of where it died instead of a 0-byte file.

Exit codes (tools/docs/RC-CONTRACT.md row 36 is the canonical register):
  0: Success / Approved / Dispatched
  1: Rejected / Vetting failed / a TARGETED issue could not be resolved (#483)
  2: Invalid arguments / CLI error
  3: Delivery / session_notify failed, or an upstream gh READ failed
  4: Capacity exhausted / No available idle lane
  5: Claim-gated -- a targeted dispatch without --force, and the issue is claimed
  6: sent-receipt-unverified -- the send may have landed and no receipt was seen
     yet. The dispatch IS recorded, so do NOT re-send. rc 2 is the ONLY code
     that proves nothing was sent.
  7: RETIRED (#760). This was the tool's OWN in-process flood guard -- a copy of
     the fleet guard that exited 7. The copy is gone; the guard now comes from
     tools/lib/oc_log.py and exits 8, like every other tool's.
  8: flood-guard (SHARED) OR targeted-landed-refused (#370) -- both leave
     NOTHING sent, told apart by their MESSAGE. Either this exact tool+args
     already failed >=4x in the last 120s (bypass OC_NO_FLOODGUARD=1, and say
     why in the lane journal), or the issue's fix has already landed (override
     with --allow-landed). A consumer branching on rc 8 must match the message,
     not the code alone.
  9: targeted-already-dispatched (#370) -- already wired to a lane and not yet
     acked, so NOTHING was sent. Override with --redispatch.

  The flood class is FLEET-WIDE (tools/lib/oc-log.sh, which pre-empts ANY tool
  and exits 8); this tool used to raise its own copy at rc 7, which is why the
  register once listed 7. Any consumer branching on rc 8 must match the message.
  6 is DISTINCT from 8/9: 6 means the dispatch IS recorded and must NOT be
  re-sent, while 8/9 prove nothing was sent -- opposite operator actions.
"""

